        <obo:IAO_0000115>A substance that augments, stimulates, activates, potentiates, or modulates the immune response at either the cellular or humoral level. A classical agent (Freund&apos;s adjuvant, BCG, Corynebacterium parvum, et al.) contains bacterial antigens. It could also be endogenous (e.g., histamine, interferon, transfer factor, tuftsin, interleukin-1). Its mode of action is either non-specific, resulting in increased immune responsiveness to a wide variety of antigens, or antigen-specific, i.e., affecting a restricted type of immune response to a narrow group of antigens. The therapeutic efficacy is related to its antigen-specific immunoadjuvanticity.</obo:IAO_0000115>
        <oboInOwl:hasDbXref>wikipedia.en:Immunologic_adjuvant</oboInOwl:hasDbXref>
        <oboInOwl:hasOBONamespace>chebi_ontology</oboInOwl:hasOBONamespace>
        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>Immunoactivator</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>
        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>Immunoadjuvant</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>
        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>Immunologic adjuvant</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>
        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>Immunopotentiator</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>
        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>Immunostimulant</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>
        <oboInOwl:id>GOCHE:50847</oboInOwl:id>
        <oboInOwl:inSubset rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/3_STAR"/>
        <rdfs:label>substance with immunological adjuvant role</rdfs:label>
    </owl:Class>
    <owl:Axiom>
